Z||000000||extinct||12-21-2001||04:19 AM||||My 88 getrag forumula has 36,000 miles on it.<BR>I notice that the clutch pedal is somewhat hard to push in.  The car shifts and drives<BR>flawlessly and very smoothly, but that darn pedal is very hard to push in, when  compared to other getrag fieros.<P>I can not see any signs of fluid leaks and it has been properly bleed.||||reg||1
Z||000001||FormulaJoe||12-21-2001||12:15 PM||||My clutch got just a bit harder to push in right before it had its little problem.  The throwout bearing was sticking on the input shaft "snout". Eventualy it stuck enough that when I pushed the clutch pedal it tore through the ears on the throwout bearing.  The ears then had a couple of small projections that every time I pushed in the clutch would rub on the diaphram spring (lovely sound).  When I redid the clutch I cleaned the snout really well and lubed the inside of the throwout bearing where it rode on the snout.  First time I used the clutch after that  I just about put my foot through the floor, difference was like night and day.  I understand that the cross shaft can develop something like what I experienced, in that case you would need to drive the cross shaft out and replace the bearings (bushings really) on the cross shaft.  I would check first to make sure that there is no restrictions on  the pedal and anything external since you do not need to pull the tranny to check it.  The other things I mentioned are internal (inside the bellhousing) and require pulling the tranny.  If your going that far, may as well do the whole clutch, unless you just enjoy pulling tha tranny   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Yellow Formula<BR>(soon to be 3.4 DOHC)||||reg||6
